    {
      "corpus": "OWASP Benchmark (Java)",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00925",
      "rule": "sql-injection",
      "title": "A constant that passes through two helpers in other files",
      "explanation": "The value comes from a helper method that returns a constant, then passes through an interface method created by a factory in another file before it is concatenated into SQL. The proof pass cannot see through the factory, so the line-level concatenation match is kept. Engine gap; most of the remaining BenchmarkJava SQL false positives have this shape."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — Rust",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00158",
      "rule": "log-injection",
      "title": "A sanitizer written as a character filter",
      "explanation": "The username is cleaned with chars().filter(|c| !c.is_ascii_control()), which removes CR and LF, but the engine does not recognise a closure-based filter as a log sanitizer, so the log line is still reported. Engine gap."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — Go",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest01167",
      "rule": "idor",
      "title": "Ownership proven by an HMAC-signed token",
      "explanation": "The handler verifies an HMAC-signed resource token that binds the caller to the resource id before the lookup. The engine recognises comparisons with the authenticated principal but not a signature check, so it reports the lookup. Engine gap."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — Ruby",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00997",
      "rule": "ssrf",
      "title": "An https-only check is labelled safe",
      "explanation": "The corpus treats a request URL as safe once its scheme is https. Restricting the scheme does not stop a request to an internal host (https://169.254.169.254/, an internal service), so we keep reporting it. A deliberate disagreement with the label."
    }
  ],
  "falseNegatives": [
    {
      "corpus": "OWASP Benchmark (Java)",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00003",
      "rule": "weak-crypto",
      "title": "A hash algorithm chosen in a properties file",
      "explanation": "The algorithm name is read with getProperty(\"hashAlg1\", ...) from benchmark.properties, where it is set to a weak hash. The engine does not resolve values from .properties files, so every hash case configured this way is missed. Engine gap; on the roadmap."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — Rust",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00125",
      "rule": "ssrf",
      "title": "A stub HTTP client",
      "explanation": "The request URL reaches simulated_reqwest_get(), a function the corpus defines to stand in for an HTTP client and which only formats a string. We report calls to real HTTP libraries, not functions that merely have a client-like name, so these cases count as misses. A deliberate choice."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — Ruby",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest01120",
      "rule": "xxe",
      "title": "Nokogiri's default parse is labelled vulnerable",
      "explanation": "Nokogiri::XML(input) does not substitute external entities by default; the corpus labels it vulnerable and labels the noent option — which turns substitution on — as safe. We follow the library's real behaviour, so Ruby XXE scores 0 on this corpus. A deliberate disagreement with the label."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "SAST Benchmark — PHP",
      "caseId": "BenchmarkTest00053",
      "rule": "csrf",
      "title": "CSRF without a session or cookie",
      "explanation": "The corpus labels a state-changing handler with no CSRF token as vulnerable even when it reads no session or cookie. Cross-site request forgery needs a credential the browser attaches by itself, so we report CSRF only for session- or cookie-authenticated handlers. A deliberate choice."
    },
    {
      "corpus": "OWASP Juice Shop",
